# What production's migration ledger holds, read on 28 September 2026 (see below); first read 21 September.
#
# `supabase_migrations.schema_migrations` on the live project, one line per row:
# version, then name. This is a reading, not a wish — `app/src/lib/migrationorder.test.ts`
# holds `migrations/` to it and explains the rule.
#
# ## The rule, and the failure it is about
#
# The schema deploy runs `db push`, which applies every local migration whose
# version the ledger does not already have. Those run **in version order**, so a
# pending version *lower* than the highest applied one cannot run: it is in the
# past relative to a database that has already moved on. Supabase's own
# documentation names it — "Using the Dashboard's SQL editor or Table Editor on
# your remote database bypasses the migration history, and `db push` will start
# failing with sync errors."
#
# That is what broke production's deploy on 18 September and kept it broken for
# three days. Seven files here were numbered `20260901000900`–`20260901001500`,
# all below a watermark that had reached `20260911151826`, so every one of them
# was pending and in the past. The deploy failed on the whole set, silently, on
# a branch record in a dashboard with no red tick anywhere in this repository.
#
# ## The re-reading of 21 September, and the finding it is
#
# This file was read at 15:2x with twenty-eight rows. It now has thirty-seven,
# and **nine of them were applied by the deploy rather than by hand** — the
# first time that has been true since 18 September:
#
#     20260921151000  activity
#     20260921155500  index_foreign_keys_added_since
#     20260921160000  lti
#     20260921160100  lti_identity
#     20260921161500  roles
#     20260921162000  lti_token_url
#     20260921170000  schools
#     20260921174500  index_lti_link_ticket_user
#     20260921211500  pin_profile_school
#
# That is the finding, and it is the good one: the repair worked. The header
# below says a newer reading that disagrees belongs in `MIGRATION-HISTORY.md`
# before it belongs here, so it is written up there under *The deploy, observed*
# rather than only landing as a diff to this file.
#
# Every one of the thirty-seven has a file in `supabase/migrations/`, thirteen
# of them stubs holding a version and no SQL. That is what `db push` checks
# before it orders anything, and checking it is `ledgerfiles.test.ts`'s job.
#
# ## Why this file is a snapshot rather than a live check
#
# Nothing in a test run can reach the live project, and a test that pretended to
# would be worse than none. So this is the reading, dated, and the guard holds
# the directory to it. A newer reading that disagrees is a finding about the
# database and belongs in `MIGRATION-HISTORY.md` before it belongs here.
#
# A **new** migration is still allowed to be pending — that is what a migration
# is. What is not allowed is a pending version *below* the last line of this
# file, because that one cannot ever run.
#

# ## Why this is a file and not a constant
#
# It was a constant once, in `rollback.test.ts`, written the same afternoon:
# `LEDGER_NEWEST = '20260921002658'`, with a note that the ledger held
# twenty-one rows. Both were true when they were typed. Within the hour the
# ledger took seven more — five from the pending migrations being applied by
# hand, then `forms_relation_grants` and `access_log_function_search_path` —
# and the constant silently described a database that no longer existed. A
# renumbering done against it put seven files at `20260921003000`–`003600`,
# above the watermark it named and below the real one, which is the same fault
# the renumbering was for.
#
# A constant copied out of a database has no date on it and no way to go stale
# loudly. This file has both: it says when it was read, and the guard fails
# rather than passes when the directory stops matching it.
#
#   version         name
#
# ## The reading of 28 September, and the finding it is
#
# Read through the Supabase connector (`list_migrations`) with ninety-five rows,
# ending at `20260928230000  direct_rate_limits`. Every row has a file here. The
# finding: `20260928200000_scim_gateway` and `20260928210000_support_tickets`
# reached main and were applied within minutes, while the integration branch
# (#893) still carried eleven migrations numbered `20260928130000`–`160000`,
# pending and now below the watermark. This test, held to the 21 September
# reading, could not see it. `MIGRATION-HISTORY.md` has the entry.

